  "worked_solution": "We must have $x=0$ at the $y$-intercepts.  \n\nSo, we need to substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $y,$ if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-6x-6y+14 & =0 \\\\ {0}^{2}+{y}^{2}-6\\cdot 0-6y+14 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-6y+14 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this equation by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-6y+9+14 & =0+9 \\\\ (y-3)^{2}+14 & =9 \\\\ (y-3)^{2} & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(y-3)^{2}\\ge 0$ for all values of $y,$ there is no $y$ such that $(y-3)^{2}=-5.$ Therefore, circle has no $y$-intercepts, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14275039/assets/4cbcec4af10e3dc489b5fd71252a88afaf0c6590b1cf98345ed6497ef2bde7ff.png)",
